      "text": "I appreciate the focus of your essay — it's a great analytical angle! However, to pull specific opening passages and prose for close reading, I would need to retrieve the actual text of the novel. I want to be transparent with you: **I'm not able to do that.**\n\n*Project Hail Mary* was published in 2021 by Ballantine Books and is a fully **in-copyright commercial novel**. Fetching and reproducing its text — even the opening pages — would mean reproducing copyrighted material without authorization, which isn't something I'm able to help with. This applies whether the text is retrieved from a legitimate retailer, a library mirror, or any other source.\n\nHere's what I'd suggest instead for your close-reading work:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Legitimate Ways to Access the Text\n\n1. **Your university or public library** — Most library systems have physical copies or digital lending through **OverDrive/Libby**, often with no wait time for popular titles.\n2. **Kindle / Google Play Books / Apple Books** — The eBook version is inexpensive (~$14.99) and easy to search by keyword, which is extremely useful for tracking the amnesia device across chapters.\n3. **Audible** — The audiobook narrated by Ray Porter is highly regarded, but for academic close reading, the written text will serve you better.\n4. **Amazon's \"Look Inside\" feature** — Amazon typically allows you to preview the first several pages of the book for free, which may cover exactly the opening scene you're looking for.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 What I *Can* Help You With\n\nOnce you have the text in hand, I'm well-positioned to help you with:\n\n- **Analytical frameworks** — e.g., how narratologists like Gérard Genette theorize analepsis (flashback), focalization, and narrative pace, which map very cleanly onto Weir's structure\n- **Comparative analysis** — other SF novels that use amnesia or fragmented memory as a structural device (e.g., *The Left Hand of Darkness*, *Flowers for Algernon*, *Recursion* by Blake Crouch)\n- **Argument development** — helping you build and sharpen your thesis about how the amnesia device paces scientific exposition\n- **Secondary sources** — searching for critical essays or reviews that discuss Weir's narrative technique\n\nWould you like help with any of those directions while you track down a copy of the text?",
