      "text": "I appreciate you explaining your situation, but I have to be straightforward with you: **I can't retrieve or reproduce those passages**, regardless of the circumstance.\n\n*Project Hail Mary* is a fully in-copyright commercial novel (Ballantine Books, 2021), and reproducing substantial portions of its text — even two specific scenes — would constitute copyright infringement. The fact that you personally own borrowing rights through your campus library doesn't change that; the restriction is about reproduction and distribution of the text, not about your access to it.\n\nI want to be honest rather than just attempt the retrieval and see what happens — that wouldn't be the right approach here.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ What You Can Do Right Now, From a Library Computer\n\n1. **Remote access your library's digital holdings** — Log into your campus library portal. Many libraries offer *Project Hail Mary* via **OverDrive/Libby** or **Hoopla** as an eBook or audiobook you can access instantly from any browser, no physical book needed.\n\n2. **Amazon's \"Look Inside\"** — Amazon's free preview typically covers the first ~20 pages, which should include the opening amnesia scene you need.\n\n3. **Ask a librarian** — Campus librarians are excellent at locating digital access to specific texts quickly, especially for academic work.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 What I *Can* Do for You Right Now\n\nEven without the text in front of you, I can meaningfully help you prepare your analysis:\n\n- **Describe the narrative structure** of those scenes based on what's publicly known (from reviews, interviews, and summaries), so you know exactly what to look for when you open the book\n- **Suggest specific rhetorical and narratological concepts** to apply to Weir's prose style in those passages\n- **Help you draft your analytical framework** so that when you do have the text, you can move straight into writing\n\nWould you like me to help in any of those ways?",
